        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          We'll be as direct here as we were at the top of this review:
          most people reading this should skip the Platinum Club Series. It
          is discontinued, so even buyers who want this tier are usually better
          served by a new Club Series+.
          If price is a meaningful factor in your decision at all, it's not
          the right machine — the <a href="/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/">Life
          Fitness T3</a> delivers the core commercial-grade durability
          advantage for roughly a quarter of the cost, and that gap buys a
          lot of other home gym equipment. If this would be your first
          treadmill, there's no way to know yet whether you'll actually use
          it enough to justify this tier of investment — buy something more
          modest first, build the habit, and consider upgrading later once
          you know your usage pattern. And if you're drawn to it mainly
          because of the price tag itself, as a status object rather than
          for the running experience, that's a reason to slow down and
          reconsider, not a reason to buy.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The Life Fitness Platinum Club Series was exactly what it claimed to
          be: club-grade equipment sold for home use. It is now discontinued,
          and Life Fitness's own replacement, the Club Series+, matches it on
          motor rating, running surface, speed, incline and user rating while
          being sold new at $7,599 to $10,999. For the small number of buyers building a
          serious dedicated home gym, a used Platinum Club Series makes sense
          only at a clear discount to that, with its console and condition
          checked.
        </p>
        <p>
          For nearly everyone else reading this review, our honest advice is
          to look at the <a href="/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/">Life Fitness
          T3</a> instead. It captures the actual engineering advantage that
          makes Life Fitness worth considering — the commercial-grade AC
          motor and fixed-frame construction — without the aspirational
          price tag attached to the extra deck length, motor headroom, and
          premium touchscreen most households will never fully use. Buy the
          Club Series+ or a used Platinum Club Series because you specifically
          want what they offer, not because they are the most expensive options
          on the page.
        </p>
